## Purple Session Enforcement

Phoenix Security ships three layers of security control for this repository, in this pack and on
the Phoenix Security platform. They are not interchangeable, and only one of them cannot be
bypassed locally:

| Layer | Artefact | Runs against | Can be bypassed by |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Advisory** | This `CLAUDE.md` block, `.claude/rules/purple-scan-enforcement.md`, and the `purple-scan` skill | Nothing — it is guidance text only | The model simply not following it |
| **B. Automatic** | The `PreToolUse` hook wired from `.claude-hooks-gate.json` (`scripts/purplephx-gate.sh`), which runs before `git commit`, `git push`, and `gh pr create` | The model, automatically, with no agreement required — but it is still local code | The developer deleting the hook or the script |
| **C. Enforced** | Server-side PR policy on the Phoenix Security platform | The human submitting the change | Nothing available from this machine |

**The word "enforced" describes layer C only.** Layer A is advisory text competing with every
other token in the model's context — the model can, and sometimes will, ignore it. Layer B runs
automatically and can genuinely block a commit or push from completing, but it is still a script
installed in this project: a developer who removes the hook, the script, or `.claude/settings.json`
removes the control along with it, which is why this pack calls it Automatic and reserves the
other word for layer C. Only layer C survives the developer, because it does not run on the
developer's own machine at all — it evaluates the pull request on the server, after it has been
pushed.

If a control needs to hold even when the person working in this repository would rather it
didn't, it must be configured as a server-side PR policy (layer C). Nothing in this pack — not
this file, not the hook, not the rule — can provide that guarantee by itself.

See `.claude/rules/purple-scan-enforcement.md` for what the installed hook actually does and how
to work with it, and the Enforcement tab in the Phoenix Security dashboard for the currently
configured mode, severities, and domains.
